6.00  2026-10-18

  - Problem 48 saved-page route normalization: return canonical flat ASCII
    ids directly rather than running trimming and prefix-removal expressions.
    Red-first Docker tests require this fast path and preserve the existing
    normalization for nested, prefixed, whitespace-bearing, Unicode,
    undefined, and reference inputs. The latest full gate passes 285 files / 25,326
    tests with all four lib/ coverage metrics at 100.0%.
    Three paired route comparisons retained the exact response hash; medians
    improved from 0.422330 to 0.347531 ms/request (1.21x). The all-routine
    50x objective remains open.

  - Problem 48 navigation rendering: concatenate ordered saved and skill nav
    fragments directly and wrap once, avoiding a temporary list array and a
    final join on the warmed route. Red-first Docker tests pin exact wrapper
    output. A 10-million-call comparison preserved output and measured 1.68x
    for the wrapper workload; route response bytes remain identical and no
    route-wide gain is claimed. The saved-nav cache-miss path also uses scalar
    accumulation instead of an intermediate array and join; a 20-fragment
    helper benchmark measured 2.09x, while the full route change was within
    noise and is not claimed as a route speedup.

  - Problem 48 web configuration snapshots: replace pretty-JSON construction
    of the private environment comparison key with versioned, length-prefixed
    framing that distinguishes missing, undefined, and defined selectors.
    Red-first Docker tests cover selector changes and delimiter-bearing values;
    nine alternating direct-method samples measured 1.34x faster. End-to-end
    seven paired route samples returned the exact same response hash, but their
    2.2% median difference was inconsistent across pairs; no route-level gain
    is claimed.

  - Problem 48 PathRegistry: cache the deepest runtime root only inside the
    explicit operation/request layer snapshot. A red-first Docker regression
    verifies one layer-inventory read across repeated scoped accesses and fresh
    results between unscoped calls. Nine alternating Docker microbench samples
    measured 1.72x for the accessor and 1.24x for the complete all_paths
    inventory with exact JSON parity. The matched saved-page route showed no
    measurable latency gain, so no route-level claim is made.

  - Problem 48: replace two repeated host-normalization substitutions with
    lowercase scheme prefix checks and one path-separator lookup. Characterized
    legacy case and URL behavior first; nine alternating Docker samples measured
    1.32x faster for the helper with exact parity. The saved-page route response
    was unchanged, and no route-level gain is claimed.

  - Problem 48 page chrome: reuse the worker-local prompt summary only after
    collector configuration and indicator freshness validation confirms the
    status is unchanged. Collector synchronization from the status route now
    invalidates the summary when configured indicator data changes, preventing
    a later page render from reusing stale text. Docker tests verify both
