4.71  2026-09-22

  - DD-1006: `_compile_launcher` in `Pax::StandaloneImage` ran a build eval
    followed by a cwd-restore eval and read `$@` once for both failure
    paths' `reason` field. Perl resets `$@` at the start and on successful
    completion of every eval, so when the build eval failed but the
    subsequent restore eval succeeded (the common case), the restore
    eval's success silently erased the real build-failure diagnostic
    before it was ever read - a failed standalone-image build reported an
    empty reason. Fixed by capturing each eval's `$@` into its own
    variable immediately after that eval returns, before the other eval
    can run and clobber it.

4.70  2026-09-22

  - DD-1005: `d2 path add`/`d2 file add` now accept a `-c`/`--create` flag
    (with an optional octal chmod mode, in bare/space-separated/=-form/
    short-flag syntax) marking an alias as lazily creatable. The first
    time anything resolves a lazy-create alias whose target is missing,
    it is created - a directory (with parents) for a path alias via
    `PathRegistry::resolve_dir`, or just the parent directory chain for a
    file alias via `FileRegistry::resolve_file` (per Q-184: a file
    alias's own target file is left absent, since the caller intends to
    write it). The create/mode metadata composes cleanly with DD-1004's
    nested skill-depth alias storage, and is fully backward compatible -
    an alias saved without `-c`/`--create` stores and reads back exactly
    as a plain path string, as it always did.

4.69  2026-09-21

  - DD-1003: `PaxCache::_run_compile_and_install`'s `md5_file` digest write
    is now atomic (write to a sibling temp path, then `rename()`), matching
    the atomic-install pattern already used for `bin_file` two lines above
    it. Previously a plain `open('>', $md5_file)` truncated the file to
    zero bytes the instant `open()` succeeded, well before the digest was
    written - any interruption in that window (SIGKILL, OOM, host reboot)
    left `md5_file` as a real, existing, zero-byte file beside an
    already-valid, already-renamed binary, permanently wedging
    `resolve()` into treating a fresh cache as stale forever.
    `t/206-paxcache-md5-atomic-write.t` proves the fix directly via a
    fork+SIGKILL race against the real `_run_compile_and_install` path.

4.68  2026-09-21

  - DD-1004: `d2 path add`/`d2 file add` now support skill-depth-prefixed
    dotted alias names (e.g. `d2 path add foo.bar.something /foobar`),
    resolving arbitrarily nested `skills/<a>/skills/<b>/...` directories.
    The write NEVER targets a skill's own `config/config.json` directly, at
    any depth, regardless of whether that skill carries its own `.git`
    (owner correction, Q-182, generalising the original git-preservation
    framing): it always walks up to the nearest git-free PARENT ancestor,
    or the global config's `skills` fallback section when none exists
    (always true for a depth-1 name, which has no parent skill) - storing
    the alias as a NESTED hash mirroring the remaining depth segments,
    never a flattened dotted string. A user's override this way always
    shadows a skill's own shipped default at read time without ever
    modifying that skill's file, letting a skill's shipped config survive
    every future `d2 skill install`/update untouched. New:
    `PathRegistry::nested_skill_dir_chain`/`nested_skill_entries`/
    `skill_config_write_location`; `Config::split_skill_alias_name`,
    `save_skill_path_alias`/`save_skill_file_alias`,
    `remove_skill_path_alias`/`remove_skill_file_alias`, and the top-level
    `save_path_alias`/`save_file_alias`/`remove_path_alias`/
    `remove_file_alias` dispatchers both CLI::Paths and CLI::Files now
    route through. An undotted alias name keeps today's exact global-config
    behavior, byte-for-byte unchanged. Added t/205-skill-depth-alias.t.

4.67  2026-09-21

  - DD-989: fixed a symlink-clobber vulnerability in
    Developer::Dashboard::IndicatorStore::set_indicator. Its staging path
    used to be the bare literal "$file.pending" with no pid/time/counter
    uniquifier at all, and permissions were secured only AFTER the write -
    the exact write-then-secure anti-pattern already fixed six other times
    (DD-599/600/601/602/848/850), but worse: a fully predictable path,
    missed by both prior sweeps because neither searched for a
    pending-file writer with no uniquifier whatsoever. Live-reproduced: a
    symlink pre-planted at the predictable staging path let a local actor
    clobber an arbitrary victim file's content and leave the real
    status.json pointing at the victim afterward. Fixed by routing through
    the shared Developer::Dashboard::PathRegistry::atomic_write_secure
    helper via a new _pending_indicator_file staging-path helper (pid +
    time + a per-process monotonic counter), matching the pattern already
    used by SessionStore.pm/Auth.pm/Collector.pm/CLI/Ask.pm/Zipper.pm.
    Added t/204-indicatorstore-pending-path-symlink.t, a regression test
    that plants a symlink at the historical predictable path and asserts
    the victim file is untouched and the real indicator file ends up a
    regular file with correct content. Updated
    docs/pending-file-staging-paths-across-the-codebase.md to add
    IndicatorStore as the 7th migrated site. Filed DD-999 (pre-existing
    500-line module-size overage) and DD-1000 (unaudited lock-file symlink
    exposure, low severity) as separate, out-of-scope follow-ups found
    during this ticket's audits.

4.66  2026-09-21

  - DD-993: fixed a CI-only, warnings-FATAL build failure ("Prototype
    mismatch: sub Developer::Dashboard::RuntimeManager::sleep (;$) vs
    (;@)") in t/100-runtimemanager-coverage.t and
    t/106-runtimemanager-coverage-2.t. RuntimeManager.pm imports its
    "sleep" from Time::HiRes, so RuntimeManager::sleep is an alias whose
    prototype is whatever the running Perl's Time::HiRes gives sleep() -
    "(;$)" on the Perl 5.44 GitHub Actions used, "(;@)" on the Perl 5.40.1
    this project's own Docker image bundles, which is why the bug was
    invisible locally. Both coverage files installed their test-only mock
    of RuntimeManager::sleep as a permanent (non-local) typeglob
    assignment guarded only by "no warnings 'redefine'", which does not
    cover Perl's separate 'prototype' warning category - even an
    unprototyped override mismatches "(;$) vs none" once only 'redefine'
    is silenced. Fixed by silencing both categories together
    ("no warnings qw(redefine prototype);") and dropping the mock's own
    explicit prototype. Added
    t/203-runtimemanager-sleep-mock-prototype.t, a deterministic,
    Perl-version-independent regression test for the mechanism, and a new
    system-scoped section in docs/expected-warnings-in-tests.md on
    overriding subs imported (not locally declared) from another module.

